It *IS* fairly well organized as is.

>WHAT IS NEEDED MOST OF ALL is a guide to nethack programmers about nethack
>source code, its c-files, header-files, data structures, etc. What I have
>heard from the development team, no-one is familiar with the whole code..

OF COURSE not. Do you think a 100,000 line program is EVER understood
by a single person ? We just do what we're good at, and have a set way
of interfacing, just like large professional development groups.

>in the code. Remember, no amount of testing can be better than properly
>designed code!

The code was heavily rewritten and ironed out in 3.0 as compared to 2.3

We also created the now fairly well organized directory structure and
segmentation of the modules. (Although I wasn't in at that time)

For each new machine, we have some separate files (i.e. for terminal
I/O, arguments/main loop and file I/O) but most of the code is
constant. The machine specific things isn't the large part, the
game mechanics is. I can't see a way of reducing the code - C++
might be nice with inheritance, but then, the same code would be
used, only differently organized. And the ration of C : C++ availability
for people may very well be 100 : 1 - remember, one of the major goals
of the game is to make it run on as many different systems as possible.

NetHack may very well be one of the largest, most widespread, most
played single games on earth. Think about that, it's really amazing !

